WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.610
- [Derek] On the morning
of the 17th of April, 2013,

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:06.270
the journal Annals of Mathematics
received a curious email.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.240
It claimed to contain a 50
page proof relating to one

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.540
of the oldest unsolved
problems in mathematics.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.300
A problem that great
mathematician Edmund Landau

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.923
called unattackable.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.120
But this proof didn't come
from a famous professor,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.640
it came from an unknown.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.200
(bright music)

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.570
Someone who had once spent years working

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.010
at a subway restaurant.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.360
- So, they're like, okay,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.430
surely this isn't gonna
work out, but whatever.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.470
You know, we'll send it to a referee.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.710
- [Derek] They expected to
find a mistake in an afternoon,

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.090
but they didn't.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:40.470
So, they went through it again,

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.480
closely studying the fragile parts

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.540
where a proof like this
normally falls apart,

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.760
but still nothing.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.490
Soon they realized they were
witnessing a breakthrough.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.620
- Oh, damn, he did it.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.330
- So, how did he do It?

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.030
What did the experts miss
and what was the problem

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.740
he was working on?

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.970
He was working on a new way

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.610
to attack one of the
hardest unsolved problems

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:03.630
in number theory,

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.403
the twin prime conjecture.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:07.733
The twin primes

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:10.200
are prime numbers separated
by just one number,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.650
like 11 and 13 or 17 and 19.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.290
As you go up, the number
line, primes become rarer

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.960
and twin primes become rarer still.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.760
But the twin prime conjecture claims

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.093
that there are infinitely many
of them you never run out.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:26.940
But is it true?

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.650
Well, one way to approach this problem

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.800
is to look at the gaps
between consecutive primes

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.381
as you go up the number line.

00:01:33.381 --> 00:01:34.214
(bright music)

00:01:34.214 --> 00:01:36.360
Now, at first they seem chaotic,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.813
but if you average them
out, a clear trend emerges.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.920
The average gap between
two primes grows roughly

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.490
as the natural logarithm of the number N.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.110
So, for example, the average gap

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:53.596
between primes around
100, is approximately 4.6.

00:01:53.596 --> 00:01:57.420
The average gap between
primes around 1000 is 6.9.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.190
Logarithms grow very slowly,

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.590
but they do keep growing forever.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.450
So, as N approaches infinity,

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:07.770
the average gap between
primes also goes to infinity,

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.840
which is not particularly encouraging

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.930
if you expect to always
be able to find primes

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.613
that are just two apart.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.610
But if you start checking large numbers,

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.597
then after a million you
quickly find the twin primes,

00:02:20.597 --> 00:02:23.523
1,000,037 and 1,000,039.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:28.800
Past a billion, there's
1,000,000,007 and 1,000,000,009.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.740
In fact, we have found a
twin prime that's as large

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:38.670
as 2,996,863,034,895

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.450
times two to the power of 1,290,000

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.490
plus or minus one.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:51.120
That is a pair of numbers
each 388,342 digits long.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.360
If you wanted to print those
in a book for some reason,

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.263
you would need around
260 pages per number.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.160
All of this is to say that
as far as we've looked,

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.620
we've kept finding twin primes.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.630
But of course, that is not how you solve

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:07.620
the twin prime conjecture,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.750
because you cannot physically check

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.520
all the numbers out to infinity.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.860
So, we need another way.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.480
And around 100 years ago,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.670
it felt like mathematicians
were getting close

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.310
with a more sophisticated method.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.440
In 1923, English mathematicians,

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.200
Hardy and Littlewood figured
out how you can estimate

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.420
how many twin primes there should be.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.880
To do it, they started with one
of the crowning achievements

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.310
of number theory, the
prime number theorem,

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:33.780
which tells you that the odds

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.940
of a large number near N being prime

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.240
are roughly one over the
natural logarithm of N.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.303
So, let's do a quick example
to see how they use this.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:47.590
Say you wanna find the
odds that 137,037 is prime.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:49.170
Well, you just plug that in

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.300
and find that it has about
an eight and a half percent

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.340
chance of being prime.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:54.870
Of course, you could use the same trick

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.830
to find the odds that 137,039 is prime,

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:01.193
which is also around 8.5%.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:05.220
So, what are the odds that
both of them are prime?

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.470
Well, you just multiply
those odds together,

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.770
that gives you around a 0.7% chance.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.630
Now we are simplifying a little here

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.180
because we're assuming that
primes are independent,

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.440
which they're not.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.030
But putting that to one side,

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.520
the odds of both a large number N

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.380
and N plus two being prime,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.400
is one over ln(n) times
one over ln(n) plus two,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.430
for large N the plus two is insignificant.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.250
So, we get the odds of any pair of numbers

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.110
of size N being a twin prime

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:37.113
are roughly one over ln(n) squared.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.140
But of course, the
problem with this approach

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.290
is that the odds of a pair
being a twin prime decrease

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:44.820
as you go to larger numbers.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.100
So, to count all twin primes up to N,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.740
we add up the odds at
every single position.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.080
In other words, we
integrate this expression

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.290
from the first prime
two up to that number.

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:58.170
Now Hardy and Littlewood
refine this argument further

00:04:58.170 --> 00:04:59.490
to account for the fact that primes

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.530
aren't really independent.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.770
But all that added is just
this correction factor upfront.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.625
So, the final expression looks like this.

00:05:07.625 --> 00:05:08.760
(bright music)

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.620
Now let's plot how many twin primes,

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.360
this predicts up to 1 trillion.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:13.860
We see that it keeps growing

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:16.380
and if we fill in the actual count,

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.640
you'll notice that it is extremely close

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:21.960
to the point where by 1 trillion,

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:26.028
our estimate is only off by 0.001%.

00:05:26.028 --> 00:05:29.070
(bright music)

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:33.450
- The only issue though, is
that this is just an estimate

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:36.330
or what mathematicians call a heuristic.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.780
It can tell you roughly how
many twin primes to expect,

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.570
but it can't guarantee that they never stop.

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:45.390
As Terry Tao puts it, for all we know,

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.400
there could be this fast conspiracy

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.550
that every time a number
N decides to be prime,

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.500
it has some secret agreement

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:54.810
with its neighbor N plus two saying

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:57.150
you're not allowed to be prime anymore.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:02.150
So, what we need is a rigorous
airtight mathematical proof,

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:03.570
but finding such a proof

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.243
it turns out, is extremely difficult.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:08.730
One of the first mathematicians

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:12.650
to really try was a 29-year-old
Norwegian Viggo Brun.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:14.100
(bright music)

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.170
Brun was working during the early years

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:17.730
of the first World War,

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.110
and with travel disrupted

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:22.050
and Europe's mathematical
centers largely out of reach,

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:26.013
much of his work happened in
quiet isolation back in Norway.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.570
His goal was simple to try
and prove that the number

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.510
or count of twin primes always increases.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:37.200
And to do this, he took a 2000
year old prime counting tool

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.870
and tried to adapt it for twin primes.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.940
Now the normal version
of this tool is called

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:46.189
the sieve of Eratosthenes, and
it works something like this.

00:06:46.189 --> 00:06:47.220
(bright music)

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.160
Say we want to find the primes up to 100.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.190
First we remove one
because that's not prime.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.557
Then we circle the first
prime, that is two,

00:06:55.557 --> 00:06:58.260
and we remove all of its multiples.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:06:59.610
We do the same for three.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:02.174
Circle it and remove its multiples,

00:07:02.174 --> 00:07:03.390
(bright music)

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:05.613
and then repeat that for five and seven.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.690
And with these four very quick operations,

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.930
every single number that's
left on your 10 by 10 grid

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.000
is a prime number.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.370
Now we stop the sieve at seven,

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:21.000
and we could keep going sieve by 11 or 30,

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.250
but it turns out that we don't have to.

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:24.423
So, why is that?

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.420
Well, the next prime is 11.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.670
So, let's check the numbers below 100

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.830
that are divisible by 11.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.750
22 is two times 11,

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:36.600
but since it's divisible by
two, we already caught it.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:39.720
We also have 33, but that
is divisible by three.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.530
And a similar logic holds for 55 and 77.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:46.080
But what about 11 times 11?

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:51.080
Well notice that is 121,
which is not in our range.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:54.630
So, every multiple of 11 below 100

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:57.360
was already caught by a smaller prime.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.720
So, sieving by 11 would
cross out nothing new.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:02.400
- So, Eratosthenes,

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:05.070
has this idea that you
only need to sieve out

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:06.690
the primes that are at most square root,

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:08.160
the size of the number.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:11.730
- But there's also a second
wave to visualize this sieve.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:13.020
Take the number line

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:16.560
and let each prime send out a
wave with the wavelength equal

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:18.240
to the size of that number.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.000
So, the wave from two lands
on every second number

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.416
and the wave from three
on every third and so on.

00:08:24.416 --> 00:08:26.010
(bright music)

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:29.040
Now, notice that wherever a wave lands,

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.203
that number has to be composite.

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:34.680
But the numbers that escape
every preceding wave,

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.813
well those have to be prime.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.010
And so you end up with this
beautiful visualization

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:42.369
of the sieve.

00:08:42.369 --> 00:08:43.740
(bright music)

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.950
And both of these methods
make it incredibly easy

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:49.410
to identify the primes.

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:52.800
- But Brun didn't care about
which primes were left,

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.470
he just needed to know how many were left.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:58.110
And to do this, he counted all the numbers

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:00.360
that were crossed out and then subtracted

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:01.950
this from the total,

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:05.640
since whatever is not
crossed out must be prime.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:09.150
So, let's use this to find the
number of primes below 100.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:10.710
We can keep a running count.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:12.660
So, we'll start with 100.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:14.460
We're gonna throw out
all the multiples of two.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:16.083
Well, that's 50 numbers left, okay?

00:09:16.083 --> 00:09:18.450
Then we're gonna throw
away multiples of three.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:19.590
Well, how many multiples of three

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:20.423
are there less than 100.

00:09:20.423 --> 00:09:22.590
100 divided by three
is not a whole number.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:26.400
It's like 33 and a third, but
we don't throw out fractions.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.590
So, okay, forget that
stupid little fraction.

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:30.960
We do the same for the
multiples of five and seven,

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:33.330
but now the count has gone negative.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:36.090
Well, it turns out that some
numbers got crossed out twice,

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:39.390
like six for example, it's
a multiple of two and three.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:42.450
So, we crossed it out once when
we removed multiples of two

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:44.970
and again when we removed
multiples of three.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:46.740
So, we have to add it back once.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:48.630
The same thing happens
for multiples of 10,

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:50.220
which is two times five,

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:51.780
14 which is two times seven,

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:54.420
15, which is three times five and so on.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:56.640
So, we add these overlaps back

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.100
and now the count rises to 28,

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:01.140
but it's still not quite right.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:04.560
- Now we've triple added
back in the multiples

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.510
of two times three times five,

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:08.730
so that we need to subtract one more time.

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:10.680
- The same thing happens for 42,

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.420
which is two times three times seven,

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:15.030
and 70, which is two
times five times seven.

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:17.820
There's a fourth triple two,
three times five times seven,

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:21.390
which is 105, but that's
already bigger than 100.

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:23.460
So, it contributes nothing here.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.880
But we'll write it in to
keep the pattern complete.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.520
After the triples, we
add back the quadruple,

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:32.760
two times three times five
times seven, which is 210.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:34.170
That's also bigger than 100,

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.720
so it contributes zero as well.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.140
And finally, we add back the
four sieving primes themselves

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:43.890
and subtract one because it
was included in the count.

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:47.130
So, the count lands at 25 primes.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:48.810
This process of alternating

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.150
between subtracting and
adding numbers back in

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:53.490
is called inclusion exclusion.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:54.960
And it makes it super easy to find out

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:58.530
how many primes there are
up to some given number.

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:01.920
Now this equation still looks
a little bit like a mess,

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:05.430
but actually we can
rearrange it into this form.

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:07.500
Now notice that every prime factor

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.980
we added just adds an
additional term in this series.

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:13.200
We've got the two that goes over here,

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:16.200
the three goes over here,
five goes over here,

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.330
and seven goes over here.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.360
And if we were trying to find
primes for some higher number

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.400
and we wanted to add 11 as a prime factor,

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:27.720
it would just add one additional
one minus one over 11 term.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:28.590
Now in general,

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:31.170
if we keep the sieve
going up to some prime P,

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:33.090
which is less than the square root of N,

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:35.643
then the count becomes approximately this.

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:40.890
- But this is still just
the count of primes.

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:43.907
What Brun really wanted was
the count of twin primes.

00:11:43.907 --> 00:11:47.430
And so this is the part
where he adapted his sieve,

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:51.360
to find a case where both
N and N plus two are prime.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:53.820
For example, when you're sieving by five,

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:56.790
you remove the numbers where
N is divisible by five,

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:58.230
just as before.

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:00.450
But in addition, you need
to remove the numbers

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.570
where N plus two is divisible by five.

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:07.800
So, you also remove 23, 28, 33 and so on

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:12.800
because in those cases, N plus
two is 25, 30, 35 and so on.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:16.140
So, this means that while
in the ordinary sieve

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:19.860
a prime P removes about
one out of every P numbers,

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:22.500
the twin prime sieve removes two.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:25.410
And this changes the count to this,

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:28.500
where the numerator for
all the terms after two,

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:30.183
ends up getting a two.

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:34.650
Now, if we plot the count of
twin primes that this predicts,

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:36.870
we see that it grows roughly like N

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:39.660
over the natural logarithm of N squared,

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.340
just as for our heuristic.

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:43.890
And so it seems like Brun did it,

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:47.490
but unfortunately that is not the case.

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:51.210
- We said that 100 divided
by three is 33, and a third,

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:52.500
but forget about that little error,

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:54.270
those little errors,

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:56.370
it's very difficult to
control those errors

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:58.110
'cause there are so many
of those little errors.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:00.960
Each one of them is at most
one it's a rounding error.

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:03.360
- Take the traditional
sieve of Eratosthenes again,

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.430
when we're sieving with just one prime,

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:07.260
we just have an over two.

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:09.120
So, one rounding error,

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:10.770
with two primes, two and three,

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:13.140
there are three separate rounding errors,

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:15.540
but with three primes,
two, three, and five,

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:18.780
there are already seven
separate rounding errors,

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:22.350
each for one term in
the inclusion exclusion.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:25.290
And so you might start to
see the general pattern here.

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:28.710
One prime gives two to the
power one minus one terms,

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:31.500
two gives two to the
power two minus one terms,

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:33.240
and three primes gives two

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:35.640
to the power three minus one terms.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:38.370
In general, if you sieve by K primes,

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:42.630
you get roughly two to the
power K minus one error terms,

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:43.800
but we can drop the one

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:47.040
because it doesn't really
make much of a difference.

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:49.650
So, that means that if you're
sieving for normal primes,

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:53.160
the error grows roughly
like two to the power K.

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:56.370
But if you're saving for twin
primes, it gets even worse.

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:59.940
And now the error grows roughly
like four to the power K.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:03.622
And so this causes a lot of
trouble very, very quick.

00:14:03.622 --> 00:14:06.300
(bright music)

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:10.173
And then here for the twin
primes, it's even worse.

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:14.193
The main term grows a bit more slowly,

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:18.630
but the error term grows much faster.

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:21.120
So, it still starts off slow.

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.350
And so you can quickly see

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:25.020
that once these error
terms start dominating,

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:26.190
you get into trouble.

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:28.950
So, the issue he was facing
is he had this main term

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:30.390
and the main term, you know,

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.310
it grew in the way he wanted to,

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:34.110
but then it just got overtaken

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:35.640
and dominated by those error terms.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:36.473
- Exactly.

00:14:36.473 --> 00:14:37.920
And that's all of analysis,

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:40.200
in general analytic number
theory in particular

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:41.640
is the fight between the main term

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:44.190
that you think is the truth,
if it actually is the truth,

00:14:44.190 --> 00:14:47.790
and getting the error
term to be provably lower order.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:49.080
- So, for twin primes,

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:51.390
the main term grows roughly like this,

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:55.320
something like N over the
natural logarithm of N squared.

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:56.670
But that's not the true count,

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:57.900
because to get the true count,

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:00.660
we must add and subtract the error terms,

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:03.960
which gives us this upper and lower bound.

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:05.550
And now you can see the problem.

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:08.340
Because the true count can
be anywhere in this range,

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:11.850
and as you can see, a big
swath of it is negative.

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:14.160
So, to prove the
conjecture, what we must do

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:16.500
is we must show that this lower bound

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:19.290
is always positive and growing.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:21.780
But that is where we run into a problem

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:23.790
with the sieve we've been using.

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:25.950
Because the more primes we sieve by,

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:29.010
the more those error
terms start to accumulate

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:30.900
and we just can show this.

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:33.870
- And so what Brun eventually realized

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:35.670
is that if you weaken the sieve,

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:37.980
if you don't sieve all the
way up to square root X,

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:40.110
but to up to something less than that,

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:44.430
and you are very careful about
your inclusion exclusion,

00:15:44.430 --> 00:15:46.440
you can actually take
care of those error terms.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:48.840
- So, run a sieve by fewer factors,

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:52.380
only up to N to the power one over 10.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:53.430
And as a result,

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:56.220
he gained enough control
over the error term.

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:58.500
But this chooses a trade off.

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:00.810
Say you wanted to find
all the prime numbers

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:02.070
up to 10 billion,

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:03.870
then using the square root method,

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:06.810
you would need to sieve up to
the square root of 10 billion,

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:08.760
which is 100,000.

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:12.090
But that gives you a lot of error terms.

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:13.890
But with Brun's method,

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:16.260
you would only need to
sieve up to 10 billion

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:20.220
to the power one over
10, which is just 10.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:21.630
But there's a catch.

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:24.000
Because Brun, didn't
sieve by all the primes,

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:26.790
there were some survivors
that weren't prime at all,

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:29.040
but numbers with many prime factors,

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.083
in Brun's case up to nine.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:34.080
So, what he actually ended up proving

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:36.180
is that there are infinitely many pairs

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:37.860
of numbers two apart,

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:41.253
where each number has at
most nine prime factors.

00:16:42.270 --> 00:16:45.990
- Brun's techniques were improved
and improved and improved,

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:49.050
and we went from nine prime
factors to seven prime factors

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:50.670
to three prime factors.

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:52.800
Until in 1973,

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:56.070
a Chinese mathematician
Chen Jingrun comes along

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:58.680
and proved that there are
infinitely many prime P,

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:02.910
where P plus two has at
most two prime factors.

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:05.520
- This is as close as you could get

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:07.710
to proving the twin prime conjecture

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:09.180
without actually getting there.

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:12.840
We're stuck like we're
like as close as we can get

00:17:12.840 --> 00:17:14.070
and no further.

00:17:14.070 --> 00:17:14.903
- Right.

00:17:14.903 --> 00:17:17.040
So, that's one approximation,

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:19.770
one mechanism for
approximating twin primes.

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:20.880
There's another.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:24.480
So, the other mechanism
is what's the smallest gap

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:25.890
between two consecutive primes?

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:27.870
So, instead of saying they differ by two

00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:30.030
and one of them's prime
and the other one, well,

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:32.280
we're gonna try to get,
you know, reduce the number

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:33.390
of prime factors that it has.

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:36.630
Instead we're gonna say
both numbers must be prime,

00:17:36.630 --> 00:17:38.793
but let's reduce the
distance between them.

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:42.390
- Now remember, on average
consecutive primes sit

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:45.240
about the natural logarithm of N apart.

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:48.120
And so the question became,
can we at least prove

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:51.153
that primes come closer
than that average gap?

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:55.140
For decades, mathematicians
kept chipping away

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:56.040
at this problem.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:58.770
By 1988, the gap had
dropped all the way down

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:01.350
to roughly a quarter of the average gap.

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:03.960
This means that say the average gap is 100

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:05.340
at some enormous scale,

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:07.260
then primes must sometimes come

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:09.543
within about 25 of each other.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:14.280
But then in 2005, Goldston,
Pintz and Yildirim,

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:17.340
proved a result that shocked
the mathematical community.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:20.490
- And they announced the
proof of a spectacular result,

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:24.690
0% bounded gaps of 0% of the average.

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:26.100
- Wait, what?

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:27.990
- They proved that you
can make the fraction

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:29.910
as small as you want.

00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:33.240
This means the gap could
be one 10th or 100th,

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:35.910
or even 1000000th of the average gap.

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:38.460
It could be as arbitrarily
small as you like,

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:41.493
and infinitely often
primes get that close.

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:44.790
- When I was young, we didn't
know there were gaps of size,

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:46.890
less than say one 10th log X

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:50.010
or infinitely many primes pairs

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:50.940
and Goldston and Yildirim,

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:54.360
came up with a method to attack that.

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:57.900
- But the method also came
close to something even bigger,

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:01.429
an absolute bounded
gap between two primes.

00:19:01.429 --> 00:19:02.430
(bright music)

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:04.680
- And so that was like a big thing.

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:07.560
- What was the big desire to go from 0%

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:11.760
or arbitrarily small to
a concrete bounded gap?

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:13.290
- Well, because we think that the bound

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:15.720
between two consecutive primes
infinitely often is two,

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:18.960
and right now we're showing
that it's log X, and X grows.

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:21.270
- If they could get to bounded gaps,

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:25.020
then they had another method
to attack the conjecture.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:26.400
The only problem

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:30.360
was that it seemed like
their tool ran into a wall.

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:32.040
And so in 2005,

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:35.610
the American Institute of
Mathematics convened a meeting,

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:39.570
gathering all the world's
leading experts on this problem.

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:43.380
GPY, Andrew Granville,
Kannan Soundararajan,

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:46.230
all gathered for a week in California

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:48.390
with one explicit goal,

00:19:48.390 --> 00:19:51.690
to prove a bounded gap between primes.

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:53.610
- I was a young graduate student,

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:56.850
I was very lucky to get
to go to this meeting.

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:58.590
You're surrounded by
the world's top experts

00:19:58.590 --> 00:19:59.423
on this subject.

00:19:59.423 --> 00:20:01.290
We spent an entire week.

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:02.790
And the upshot of this week

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:04.140
was basically that it's impossible.

00:20:04.140 --> 00:20:07.470
And Soundararajan shows
how it's not possible

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:08.340
to do this thing.

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:11.160
And so as far as I was
concerned, that was that, and I,

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:13.140
you know, this wasn't
gonna be my thesis problem,

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:14.280
I'd have to do something else

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:16.500
and I went off and and did other things.

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:19.140
But there was one person
who was not at this meeting,

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:20.602
Yitang Zhang.

00:20:20.602 --> 00:20:22.770
(bright music)

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:24.420
- Zhang grew up in China,

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:27.120
and around the age of 30 moved to the U.S.

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:29.310
to get his PhD in mathematics,

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:31.650
but he never got any
recommendation letters

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:33.873
and so struggled to find a job.

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:38.490
He ended up living in his car for time

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:41.910
and ultimately working
odd jobs for seven years,

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:44.730
including at Subway,
where he kept the books

00:20:44.730 --> 00:20:46.293
and sorted receipts.

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:50.360
Yet, while doing all of
that in his spare time,

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:52.980
he would drive down to the local library

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:56.175
to read books and
journals on number theory.

00:20:56.175 --> 00:20:57.810
(bright music)

00:20:57.810 --> 00:21:01.500
Then in 1999, he got a lucky break.

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:04.440
One of his friends helped
him get a job as a lecturer

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:06.990
at the University of New Hampshire.

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:09.780
So, now he could spend all his time doing

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:12.180
what he loved most, math.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:14.130
Zhang said that when he was a kid,

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:15.870
he "Imagined there would be a day

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:18.627
that I would solve a major math problem."

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:21.930
And by 2010, he had identified

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:24.210
his major problem to focus on,

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:26.403
bounded gaps between primes.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:29.760
But to understand what he was working on,

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:34.760
we need to understand what
exactly it is that GPY had built.

00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:38.700
See, they wanted to find two
primes within a bounded gap.

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:41.970
And to do this, they
imagined a stencil of sorts

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:43.560
with some holes in it.

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:44.970
So, let's do the same,

00:21:44.970 --> 00:21:47.010
and for the sake of this example,

00:21:47.010 --> 00:21:49.860
let's say the stencil
has a diameter of six

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:53.490
and holes at spot zero, two and six.

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:56.700
Next we place the stencil
anywhere on the number line,

00:21:56.700 --> 00:21:58.413
say starting at 12.

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:03.600
And we ask a simple question,
how many primes do we see?

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:06.780
In this case, we see 12, 14, and 18,

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:07.860
none of which are prime.

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:09.780
So, we write down zero.

00:22:09.780 --> 00:22:13.470
Next we shift the stencil
over one spot and repeat,

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:17.010
13, 15, and 19, two of which are prime.

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:18.960
So, we write down two.

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:20.460
And then we keep doing this.

00:22:20.460 --> 00:22:21.780
If you slide it a bit further,

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:25.830
we catch two primes again, 23 and 29.

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:29.400
Now, if you keep moving the
stencil across the number line

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:31.710
and it keeps catching two primes,

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:33.150
then you have proven

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:36.030
that infinitely often
pairs of primes exist

00:22:36.030 --> 00:22:37.530
within a bounded gap.

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:42.000
Six in this case, since that's
the diameter of our stencil.

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:45.180
But there are two problems
with this approach.

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:47.550
The first is that we don't know exactly

00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:49.350
where all the primes are.

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:52.740
And the second is that of
course you can't keep sliding

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:54.840
a stencil down the number line forever

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:57.210
because well, it's infinitely long.

00:22:57.210 --> 00:22:59.580
Fortunately, there is an easy way

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:01.140
to get around that first problem.

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:04.500
See, while we don't know exactly
where all the primes are,

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:07.500
we do know how they behave on average.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:10.860
For example, say we take some
stretch of the number line

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:14.130
from some very large number X to two X,

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:16.890
then we can't predict
exactly which numbers

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:18.480
in this stretch will be prime.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:21.690
But what we can do is put this
into the prime number theorem

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:25.613
and estimate how many
primes to expect on average.

00:23:25.613 --> 00:23:27.240
(bright music)

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:30.480
And GPY realized they
could do a similar thing

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:32.130
for their stencil method

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:35.160
to build an averaging machine of sorts.

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:37.680
All you do is you set
up the stencil you want,

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:40.860
input your range, and out comes
the average number of primes

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:42.930
it caught per position.

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:46.140
So, let's do a quick
example without the machine

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:48.060
to see how this helps.

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:50.250
Let's use the same stencil from before

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:52.860
and use the range 20 to 40.

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:55.860
Now, in reality, you would
use a much larger range,

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:58.680
but for simplicity, let's stick to this.

00:23:58.680 --> 00:24:01.380
To find the average, you
just place the stencil

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:02.760
at the start of your range,

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:04.980
and you'll look at which numbers you see,

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:06.990
20, 22 and 26.

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:08.040
None of which are prime

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:10.620
and so the total prime count is zero.

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:12.840
Then we shift the stencil over one spot

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:15.900
and see 21, 23 and 27.

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:19.890
One of which is prime, so we
increase the total by one.

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:21.990
Now let's keep shifting the stencil

00:24:21.990 --> 00:24:24.540
and keep adding all the primes we see.

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:26.160
No primes, so add zero,

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:29.117
two primes, add two, and so on.

00:24:29.117 --> 00:24:31.920
(stencil shuffling)

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:33.930
Now, if you keep shifting the stencil

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:35.460
all the way until the end,

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:40.124
you find a total of 14 primes
across 21 starting positions.

00:24:40.124 --> 00:24:42.570
(bright music)

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:45.930
And so the average is 14 over 21,

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:49.953
or about 0.67 primes per location.

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:55.470
Now in this case where we
checked every spot by hand,

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:58.380
we could see that the stencil
caught several positions

00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:00.360
with two primes in them.

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:03.150
But the averaging machine
doesn't tell us this.

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:05.340
All that spits out is an average,

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:07.050
and that average alone

00:25:07.050 --> 00:25:10.470
can't guarantee that it
caught two primes at once,

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:12.960
because you could just
as well imagine spreading

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:17.340
all of these 14 primes over
14 individual positions.

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:21.360
And the same would be true
if the average was 0.8, 0.9,

00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:23.100
or even one.

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:24.570
I know it's unlikely,

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:28.800
but it could be that every
position only caught one prime

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:31.230
to bring the average to one.

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:34.410
But what if the average
was larger than one?

00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:37.950
What if we found 22 primes in total?

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:41.580
Well, even if every position
had caught one prime,

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:43.650
you would still have one prime left over

00:25:43.650 --> 00:25:47.010
and that needs to go in one
of those other positions.

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:50.220
So, in other words, if
the average is above one,

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:51.720
then that guarantees

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:55.140
that at least one position
caught two primes.

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:56.520
And so that's the game.

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:59.760
Get the average number
of primes above one,

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:02.640
but that still leaves one question.

00:26:02.640 --> 00:26:06.900
Even if you could do that, then
how does that help you prove

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:08.940
that you keep getting bounded gaps

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:10.980
all the way up to infinity?

00:26:10.980 --> 00:26:15.180
Well, remember you just proved
that for any large number X,

00:26:15.180 --> 00:26:18.300
so there's nothing stopping
you from replacing that X

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:21.780
with two X, and that two X with four X,

00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:23.970
and then you could replace
the two X with four X

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:27.170
and the four X with eight X, and so,

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:29.310
on all the way up to infinity.

00:26:29.310 --> 00:26:31.830
So, if you can prove that the average gap

00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:35.190
is above one for any large number X,

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:36.870
then with this clever setup,

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:40.470
it immediately extends all
the way out to infinity,

00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:43.109
and you would've proven your bounded gap.

00:26:43.109 --> 00:26:45.450
(bright music)

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:47.010
- Unfortunately, running the machine

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:48.540
with just the current inputs,

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:50.250
the stencil visits many positions

00:26:50.250 --> 00:26:52.320
where it catches zero primes,

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:53.790
this drags the average down

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:56.190
and means it will never get above one.

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:59.640
So, GPY made one final
tweak to their machine.

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:01.680
They realize that some starting positions

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:04.200
are more likely to catch
primes than others.

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:07.230
For example, if the stencil
only lands on even numbers,

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:11.130
it will always give zero
primes unless it includes two.

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:12.420
So, those positions

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:15.120
should not be weighted
equally to the others.

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:17.460
Similarly, if you can divide
one or more of the numbers

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:19.440
in the stencil by three, five, seven,

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:21.270
or other small prime factors,

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:24.720
it's also more unlikely you
have primes in your stencil.

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:27.390
So, they should also be weighted less.

00:27:27.390 --> 00:27:30.960
Now, GPY did a few simple checks
like this for each position

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:33.750
to assign it a weight,
and estimate of how likely

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:36.300
it is that this position contains primes.

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:38.100
This turned their averaging machine

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:40.650
into a weighted averaging machine.

00:27:40.650 --> 00:27:43.920
And as a result, that
weighted average shot up.

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:45.390
With their updated machine

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:48.390
they proved that if you
pick any large number X,

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:50.580
then in the range X to two X,

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:54.060
you could get the weighted
average arbitrarily close to one,

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:56.190
but not above one.

00:27:56.190 --> 00:27:59.730
They were close, but they ran into a wall.

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:02.190
A wall that came from their weights,

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:04.770
because to figure out how to
weigh different positions,

00:28:04.770 --> 00:28:07.050
they needed to know how
primes are distributed

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:09.990
in something known as an
arithmetic progression.

00:28:09.990 --> 00:28:12.300
These are basically just series of numbers

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:15.000
where they're all the
same step size apart.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:17.640
So, 3, 7, 11, 15,

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:21.090
that's an arithmetic progression
with a step size of four.

00:28:21.090 --> 00:28:24.420
Now, GPY needed to know
how primes were distributed

00:28:24.420 --> 00:28:26.700
over many such arithmetic progressions

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:29.400
with all kinds of different step sizes.

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:32.310
Fortunately, they knew
of theorem from the 1960s

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:34.410
they could use to do exactly this.

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:37.560
- It's the substitute for the
Riemann hypothesis on average,

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:40.260
and it turns out that that's
what's actually necessary

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:42.600
in a lot of these arithmetic applications.

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:45.180
- But that theorem
doesn't work everywhere.

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:48.480
It only works for step sizes
up to the square root of X

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:50.460
or X to the one half.

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:52.770
The technical term for this one half

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:55.500
is the level of distribution or theta.

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:57.840
It tells you how large the step sizes

00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:00.840
can be while still getting
a reliable count of primes

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:02.100
in those progressions.

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:03.570
And mathematicians believed

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:06.510
that the theorem worked
beyond X to the half,

00:29:06.510 --> 00:29:08.760
but this was never proven,

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:11.310
and that is where the trouble lies.

00:29:11.310 --> 00:29:14.610
When GPY ran their
calculation with this ceiling,

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:17.250
they found the maximum
weighted average they could get

00:29:17.250 --> 00:29:19.350
was two times theta.

00:29:19.350 --> 00:29:22.260
In other words, they
got really close to one,

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:25.260
but they could never actually cross it.

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:26.460
- They showed in their method

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:28.110
that if you could get beyond a half,

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:31.200
you could do 0.5000001.

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:34.710
Then instead of just getting
0% of the average gap,

00:29:34.710 --> 00:29:36.060
they can get bounded gaps.

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:38.880
The primes would be some
fixed distance apart.

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:41.370
- That's what the 2005
meeting was all about.

00:29:41.370 --> 00:29:45.060
They kept trying to push
past that one half barrier,

00:29:45.060 --> 00:29:49.140
but they ended up concluding
that it was impossible.

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:52.620
From 2010 on, Zhang spent
two years hacking away

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:56.100
at it as well, internalizing
the GPY argument,

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:59.643
working day and night just to
try and find a way through.

00:30:00.709 --> 00:30:02.310
(bright music)

00:30:02.310 --> 00:30:04.410
But by the summer of 2012,

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:08.340
Zhang was exhausted, and
had nothing to show for it.

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:12.450
Hoping to clear his head, he
visited a friend in Colorado,

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:14.490
where one evening while they were waiting

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:15.870
to leave for concert,

00:30:15.870 --> 00:30:19.680
Zhang stepped outside
alone into the backyard,

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:23.100
looking out for the deer that
usually roamed the property,

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:24.663
but no deer came.

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:28.920
So, he was just walking and thinking

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:31.953
when all of a sudden
the answer came to him.

00:30:33.180 --> 00:30:35.550
He hadn't brought any notes or paper,

00:30:35.550 --> 00:30:38.703
but Zhang believed his idea would work.

00:30:38.703 --> 00:30:41.040
(bright music)

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:42.870
- You see, GPY had to count primes

00:30:42.870 --> 00:30:44.670
in many different arithmetic progressions

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:46.860
with all sorts of step sizes.

00:30:46.860 --> 00:30:49.920
But Zhang focused on a
special class of step sizes,

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:52.530
one's built only from small prime factors,

00:30:52.530 --> 00:30:55.110
and he realized he could
reorganize the error terms

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:58.620
into a form where most
of them get canceled out.

00:30:58.620 --> 00:31:00.960
This allowed him to push
past the half barrier

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:05.283
by a tiny fraction, just one over 584.

00:31:06.120 --> 00:31:09.120
Zhang spent the next year
finalizing his proof,

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:11.550
and then on the 17th of April, 2013,

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:15.273
he sent off that curious email
to the Annals of Mathematics.

00:31:16.350 --> 00:31:17.430
- Now, the Annals gets a proof

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:20.370
of the Riemann Hypothesis every other day,

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:23.100
so they're like, okay, surely
this isn't gonna work out,

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:23.933
but whatever.

00:31:23.933 --> 00:31:25.800
You know, we'll send it to a referee.

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:28.470
And they're like, well,

00:31:28.470 --> 00:31:30.240
let's spend a few hours this afternoon.

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:31.410
We'll find a mistake.

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:33.390
We'll tell the annals, you
know, sorry, it didn't work out.

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:35.940
Here's where the the gap is.

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:37.470
And they start reading it,

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:39.270
and then they flip back,
because they're experts.

00:31:39.270 --> 00:31:41.160
So, they can just flip through
like, yeah, this'll work,

00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:42.450
this will work, but wait a second,

00:31:42.450 --> 00:31:43.890
you're gonna get stuck here.

00:31:43.890 --> 00:31:46.140
And they flip five pages
and they're like, oh,

00:31:46.140 --> 00:31:46.980
that's interesting.

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:48.510
That's how you're gonna handle that.

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:50.640
Okay, but then you're gonna have another,

00:31:50.640 --> 00:31:53.182
it's like trying to lay down a carpet

00:31:53.182 --> 00:31:54.533
in a room.

00:31:54.533 --> 00:31:56.070
You're like, okay, I know that corner,

00:31:56.070 --> 00:31:57.300
that corner's gonna screw you up

00:31:57.300 --> 00:31:59.490
even if you managed to
make it work over there.

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:01.500
And then he goes over there,
no, he cut it just right.

00:32:01.500 --> 00:32:02.370
It fits in that corner.

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:03.420
Wait a second.

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:05.820
And then they flip, you
know, five more pages

00:32:05.820 --> 00:32:06.690
and by the end of the week,

00:32:06.690 --> 00:32:09.440
they've reconstructed the
proof and everything's right.

00:32:10.410 --> 00:32:11.997
- Wow.

00:32:11.997 --> 00:32:12.830
(bright music)

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:16.020
- Zhang's stencil had 3.5 million slots,

00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:18.960
spread across a span of 70 million.

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:22.440
And by proving two of those
slots always catch primes,

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:26.190
he proved a bounded gap of 70 million.

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:27.120
When the news broke,

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:29.190
mathematicians were in disbelief.

00:32:29.190 --> 00:32:31.260
- It was basically an
unknown in the field.

00:32:31.260 --> 00:32:32.160
I actually thought

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:33.720
when I started reading
Yitang Zhang's paper

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:36.180
and started realizing
it was probably correct,

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:37.620
I thought it was probably
one of the people

00:32:37.620 --> 00:32:39.180
I knew under a pseudonym,

00:32:39.180 --> 00:32:41.520
trying to avoid being
embarrassed by being wrong.

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:42.870
If they were wrong.

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:45.660
- Of course it wasn't, Zhang was real.

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:46.980
And just over a year later

00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:49.875
he was awarded the MacArthur Genius Grant.

00:32:49.875 --> 00:32:51.780
(bright music)

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:54.780
- To me, this is a
really interesting aspect

00:32:54.780 --> 00:32:56.340
of mathematics in particular.

00:32:56.340 --> 00:32:58.590
I think it's one of the very few fields

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:02.400
where we have a truly honest approach

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:05.880
to success and what counts as success.

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:08.490
He sent in an argument,
people took it seriously.

00:33:08.490 --> 00:33:09.323
They looked at it.

00:33:09.323 --> 00:33:10.920
They didn't think that
he had a true proof.

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:14.340
They sat there, they gave him the time

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:15.360
that the argument was due,

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:17.820
and he was immediately made a
hero as he should have been.

00:33:17.820 --> 00:33:19.980
To me, it shows that
mathematics culture works.

00:33:19.980 --> 00:33:21.630
We're doing the right thing.

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:24.600
I think the cover of
like Scientific American

00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:27.150
or whatever in 2013 is
the story Yitang Zhang

00:33:27.150 --> 00:33:28.860
and how he got this bounded gap

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:31.710
between primes toiling
in complete isolation.

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:33.600
And maybe it's because he was in isolation

00:33:33.600 --> 00:33:35.820
that he didn't have the group
think that the rest of us did.

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:37.980
To know that this was not,

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:39.870
we were all told it's
an impossible problem.

00:33:39.870 --> 00:33:44.040
- After realizing a bounded gap
was not an impossible dream,

00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:47.460
people reworked Zhang's
method to optimize it.

00:33:47.460 --> 00:33:50.430
Terence Tao spearheaded an
online group called Polymath,

00:33:50.430 --> 00:33:52.050
and they sharpened the method.

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:54.750
So, every month, week or day,

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:56.970
that upper bound kept coming down.

00:33:56.970 --> 00:33:59.220
One of the attendees described
being at a conference

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:02.100
at the time, as I remember,
sort of day by day,

00:34:02.100 --> 00:34:04.650
everyone was refreshing
their screens to see

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:06.660
who had the world record now,

00:34:06.660 --> 00:34:08.310
and ultimately they got the number

00:34:08.310 --> 00:34:10.923
all the way down to 4,680.

00:34:12.540 --> 00:34:16.020
- Meanwhile, there's a young
postdoc named James Maynard,

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:19.020
who just got his PhD at
Oxford with Roger Heath-Brown

00:34:19.020 --> 00:34:22.800
and other one of these analytic
number theory world experts.

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:25.683
And he's working with Andrew
Granville, in Montreal.

00:34:26.850 --> 00:34:31.620
And he has a completely orthogonal
approach to this problem

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:32.453
that he'd been making

00:34:32.453 --> 00:34:34.770
some very incremental
progress on independently.

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:38.227
- When Maynard started, his
advisor explicitly told him,

00:34:38.227 --> 00:34:41.370
"I hope you won't work on
this problem full time,

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:44.517
because I'm really pretty
confident you're going to fail."

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:47.910
But Maynard ignored the warning,

00:34:47.910 --> 00:34:51.510
and came up with a different
way to attack the problem,

00:34:51.510 --> 00:34:55.320
and within just a few
months, he got it to work.

00:34:55.320 --> 00:34:57.693
Further bring down the gap to 600.

00:34:58.740 --> 00:35:01.620
But his method also proved something else.

00:35:01.620 --> 00:35:03.720
- He can get three primes
in a bounded window.

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:05.640
The bound has to change
depending on how many primes

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:06.990
you wanna put in that window,

00:35:06.990 --> 00:35:08.400
and that his number is better.

00:35:08.400 --> 00:35:11.910
And the method has nothing to
do with the exponent one half.

00:35:11.910 --> 00:35:13.110
- Wait, what?

00:35:13.110 --> 00:35:16.800
- One half was a pure mirage.

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:18.450
It was a red herring.

00:35:18.450 --> 00:35:19.590
- That is crazy.

00:35:19.590 --> 00:35:22.590
One half was not a
fundamental limit at all.

00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:26.220
See where GPY average was
stuck at two times theta,

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:29.520
Maynard's average grew
roughly like theta over two

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:31.500
times the natural logarithm of K,

00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:34.440
where K is the number
of slots in the stencil.

00:35:34.440 --> 00:35:38.103
So, all Maynard needed was
enough slots and it worked.

00:35:39.030 --> 00:35:41.340
- You need any number greater than zero.

00:35:41.340 --> 00:35:43.200
When you go up to one half
because you have one half,

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:45.060
you'll get better numerics.

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:47.730
But the bounded gaps you
could do, just going up to,

00:35:47.730 --> 00:35:50.910
you know, 0.01, not 0.50101.

00:35:50.910 --> 00:35:54.090
Now, curiously, Terry Tao independently

00:35:54.090 --> 00:35:56.430
has the same approach.

00:35:56.430 --> 00:35:57.810
He tells Ben Green about it,

00:35:57.810 --> 00:36:00.600
Ben Green is meeting
with Andrew Granville,

00:36:00.600 --> 00:36:03.690
and says, "Hey, Terry's got this new idea

00:36:03.690 --> 00:36:05.520
that he thinks is gonna get even farther."

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:06.750
And Granville, says, wait a second.

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:09.810
My postdoc, Maynard, is
doing exactly the same thing.

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:11.700
We gotta get these two guys
to talk to one another.

00:36:11.700 --> 00:36:13.170
Terry's a Fields medalist,

00:36:13.170 --> 00:36:16.740
he's like a super
heavyweight by this point,

00:36:16.740 --> 00:36:21.240
whereas James is this,
you know, fresh PhD,

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:23.760
and Terry says, you take
it, I don't need this.

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:24.690
You know, you this,

00:36:24.690 --> 00:36:26.610
it's your idea, you go with it.

00:36:26.610 --> 00:36:29.400
- By early 2014, Maynard joined forces

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:31.080
with Tao's Polymath group.

00:36:31.080 --> 00:36:34.560
- It was clear that somehow 600
was just a proof of concept,

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:37.500
and the same methods would
give something smaller,

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:40.770
but there were extra ideas
that you could maybe use

00:36:40.770 --> 00:36:42.150
to squeeze everything out.

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:43.890
And so the current world record

00:36:43.890 --> 00:36:45.690
is that there's infinitely
many pairs of primes

00:36:45.690 --> 00:36:49.108
that differ by no more than 246.

00:36:49.108 --> 00:36:49.941
(bright music)

00:36:49.941 --> 00:36:52.950
- And that for now is where it stops.

00:36:52.950 --> 00:36:56.490
In 2022, James Maynard was
awarded the Fields Medal,

00:36:56.490 --> 00:37:00.960
Mathematics, highest honor
for his work on prime gaps.

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:03.180
- Yeah, I think that's the basic outline

00:37:03.180 --> 00:37:06.060
is the kind of two
directions of approximation

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:08.670
to twin primes Chen's direction,

00:37:08.670 --> 00:37:12.750
Brun, Chen, whatever,
versus Zhang and Maynard,

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:15.000
and this story that the
Zhang wasn't at this meeting,

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:16.320
so he didn't know it was impossible.

00:37:16.320 --> 00:37:17.880
- It reminded me of the four minute mile

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:19.650
where everyone thought it was impossible.

00:37:19.650 --> 00:37:21.030
No one did it.

00:37:21.030 --> 00:37:22.680
- And then for the first time ever,

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:25.860
Roger Bannister broke it in 1954,

00:37:25.860 --> 00:37:27.780
and after knowing it was possible,

00:37:27.780 --> 00:37:29.640
just 46 days later,

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:32.970
another runner called John
Landy broke it as well.

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:37.350
And by the end of 1956,
10 people had broken it,

00:37:37.350 --> 00:37:39.693
all from knowing that it was possible.

00:37:41.490 --> 00:37:44.940
So, what about pushing
that gap down even lower?

00:37:44.940 --> 00:37:46.230
Is that possible?

00:37:46.230 --> 00:37:48.180
Well, mathematicians have found ways

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:50.100
to bring it down even more,

00:37:50.100 --> 00:37:52.920
but all of these results are conditional.

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:56.550
For example, there is the
Elliot-Halberstam conjecture,

00:37:56.550 --> 00:37:57.900
which assumes primes

00:37:57.900 --> 00:38:00.780
are spread evenly across
arithmetic progressions,

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:02.520
or that the level of distribution

00:38:02.520 --> 00:38:04.830
can be taken as large as one.

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:08.190
And in 2013, Maynard
showed that if you assume

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:09.540
this conjecture is true,

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:12.060
then the gap plummets to just 12.

00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:12.893
A year later,

00:38:12.893 --> 00:38:15.180
the Polymath Group
proved that if you assume

00:38:15.180 --> 00:38:17.790
an even stronger version
of this conjecture,

00:38:17.790 --> 00:38:21.000
the gap drops all the way down to six.

00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:25.473
But without assuming
anything, 246 still stands.

00:38:27.930 --> 00:38:30.060
Let me ask you, do you
think we're gonna solve

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:31.650
the twin Prime conjecture?

00:38:31.650 --> 00:38:36.000
- I am totally convinced that
humanity will eventually solve

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:37.410
the twin prime conjecture.

00:38:37.410 --> 00:38:40.710
Often when I'm asked about some
of these big famous problems

00:38:40.710 --> 00:38:45.120
like Twin Primes or Riemann
or something like that,

00:38:45.120 --> 00:38:48.330
one way of kind of avoiding the question

00:38:48.330 --> 00:38:52.080
is that if you imagine you
are randomly distributed in time,

00:38:52.080 --> 00:38:57.030
you'd expect to be sort of
typically roughly halfway

00:38:57.030 --> 00:38:59.490
through when the
conjectures been open for.

00:38:59.490 --> 00:39:02.340
So, an actual guess, if you
have no other information,

00:39:02.340 --> 00:39:05.460
is to guess that a problem will be open

00:39:05.460 --> 00:39:07.860
for as long as it has been open for already.

00:39:07.860 --> 00:39:09.750
But obviously this doesn't
work for twin primes,

00:39:09.750 --> 00:39:11.940
'cause we don't know whether it's-

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:12.899
- Right.

00:39:12.899 --> 00:39:13.732
(all laughing)

00:39:13.732 --> 00:39:16.890
- 125 years old or whether
it's like 2000 years old.

00:39:16.890 --> 00:39:18.600
So, I think it's a fools game to guess,

00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:21.690
but it clearly needs a really big idea,

00:39:21.690 --> 00:39:24.780
but maybe it only needs one big idea.

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:27.270
- Although maybe, just maybe,

00:39:27.270 --> 00:39:30.510
not knowing this with
certainty is for the best.

00:39:30.510 --> 00:39:34.470
Because if we knew for sure
that this was impossible,

00:39:34.470 --> 00:39:36.240
then we would've likely missed out

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:37.920
on most of these inventions

00:39:37.920 --> 00:39:40.290
and new methods over the past century.

00:39:40.290 --> 00:39:43.680
So, sometimes it pays
not to know everything.

